\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

This section details the computational, numerical, and architectural
methods used in the Aether-Core/200LY simulation environment. The
framework integrates observational data ingestion, multi-physics
numerical solvers, surrogate PINO inference, distributed GPU execution,
and anomaly-detection pipelines.

\subsection{Simulation Domain and Discretization}
The simulation volume spans a radius of 200 light-years centered on the
solar position. Spatial discretization is performed using a uniform
Cartesian grid:
\begin{equation}
\Omega = [-200, 200]^3 \; \text{LY},
\end{equation}
with resolution:
\begin{equation}
N_x = N_y = N_z = 1024.
\end{equation}

Particle-based N-body elements are evolved concurrently with grid-based
MHD fields using a hybrid particle–mesh scheme.

\subsection{Observational Data Integration}
Gaia DR3 and TESS datasets are preprocessed into:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Stellar position vectors $\mathbf{r}_i$
    \item Velocity vectors $\mathbf{v}_i$
    \item Photometric variability indicators
    \item Derived density fields $\rho(\mathbf{x})$
\end{itemize}

Data are stored in an HDF5/Zarr lake and accessed through a parallel
I/O layer optimized for multi-GPU workloads.

\subsection{Numerical Solvers}

\subsubsection{N-Body Gravitation}
Particle accelerations are computed using a Barnes–Hut octree with
opening angle $\theta = 0.7$. The softened gravitational force is:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{a}_i =
G \sum_{j \neq i}
\frac{m_j (\mathbf{r}_j - \mathbf{r}_i)}
{\left( \|\mathbf{r}_j - \mathbf{r}_i\|^2 + \epsilon^2 \right)^{3/2}},
\end{equation}
with softening length $\epsilon = 0.01$ LY.

\subsubsection{Magnetohydrodynamics}
Ideal MHD equations are solved using a second-order Godunov scheme with
HLLD Riemann solvers. Divergence cleaning is performed using:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \psi}{\partial t} + c_h^2 \nabla \cdot \mathbf{B} = 0,
\end{equation}
where $\psi$ is the cleaning field and $c_h$ is the hyperbolic cleaning
speed.

\subsection{Surrogate PINO Model}
Physics-Informed Neural Operators (PINO) are trained on high-resolution
MHD and N-body snapshots. The surrogate predicts long-horizon field
evolution:
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}(t + \Delta t) = \mathcal{F}_{\text{PINO}}(u(t)),
\end{equation}
with training loss:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} =
\lambda_{\text{data}} \|u - \hat{u}\|^2
+ \lambda_{\text{phys}} \|\mathcal{N}(u)\|^2,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{N}$ encodes PDE residuals.

\subsection{Distributed GPU Execution}
The simulation is executed across up to 1024 GPUs using:
\begin{itemize}
    \item CUDA kernels for N-body and MHD updates
    \item GPUDirect MPI for halo exchanges
    \item Unified memory fabric for cross-node tensor operations
\end{itemize}

Communication overhead is monitored:
\begin{equation}
\frac{T_{\text{comm}}}{T_{\text{total}}} < 0.12.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Chaos Injection and Fault Tolerance}
Chaos events are introduced at predefined iterations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item GPU memory pressure spike at iteration 250
    \item MPI jitter at iteration 600
\end{itemize}

Fault tolerance is validated through:
\begin{equation}
t_{\text{step}} \le 0.15 \text{ s},
\end{equation}
and checkpoint recovery consistency.

\subsection{Spectral Anomaly Detection}
Spectral intensity fields are computed from simulated plasma emission
models. The anomaly signal is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\Delta I(E) = I_{\text{obs}}(E) - I_{\text{dec}}(E),
\end{equation}
and peaks are detected via:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\Delta I}{dE} = 0, \quad
\frac{d^2\Delta I}{dE^2} < 0.
\end{equation}

Spatial localization is performed by maximizing:
\begin{equation}
\Delta I(\mathbf{x}, E = 3.55 \text{ keV}).
\end{equation}

Confidence is derived from surrogate error:
\begin{equation}
C = 1 - \frac{\text{NRMSE}}{0.05}.
\end{equation}